WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:19.840
This round little unicellular creature came
to us via a plankton net, a mesh with tiny,

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:25.930
microscopic holes through which we ran hundreds
and hundreds of liters of water, letting us

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:30.210
collect anything too large to pass through.

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:34.890
We haven’t been able to identify this species
yet, making it a bit of a mystery.

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:41.250
But the bigger mystery is still to come because
this little creature is about to undergo that

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:46.600
most universal and unknowable experience of
all—death.

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:53.359
Death comes to the microcosmos in many forms.

00:00:53.359 --> 00:00:59.640
Like this Stentor Polymorphous, slowly expelling
the contents of its once trumpet-like body

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.850
into the surrounding environment.

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:09.420
Or this dead larva, whose exoskeleton is now
an inanimate host to two unicellular organisms.

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:16.200
Even the mighty tardigrade, which has survived
as a species through multiple mass extinctions,

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:22.420
is not immune to death.

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:27.670
This is, of course, the natural order of things.

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:35.670
Predators hunt, and their prey attempts to
survive…with varying levels of success.

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:42.850
This is Loxophyllum meleagris, a large unicellular
organism that we’ve shown before eating

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:44.270
a rotifer.

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:49.470
This one is practically stuffed with those
multicellular creatures.

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:52.930
We counted five rotifers inside of it.

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:59.060
But sometimes the predator becomes the prey,
and even the Loxophyllum meleagris has to

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:05.150
find ways to ensure survival when other species
come after it.

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:09.369
This seemingly unlikely threat is the Lacrymaria
olor.

00:02:09.369 --> 00:02:17.370
Its name, in Latin means “tears of a swan”,
a name that suits both its teardrop shape

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:23.860
and its neck-like extension, which gets up
to 8 times longer than its body in search

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:25.860
of prey.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:32.660
Sometimes, we can see its neck poking out
of the dirt on our microscope slide.

00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:37.190
But even knowing that, you’d be forgiven
for thinking that it’s unlikely that something

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:41.750
so small could pose a problem for those larger
Loxophyllum.

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:50.440
And yet, the Lacrymaria manages to take quite
a chunk out of the Loxophyllum.

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:56.530
The Loxophyllum though survives thanks to
its ability to regenerate the piece that was

00:02:56.530 --> 00:03:00.540
taken, but not all prey gets so lucky.

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:08.870
Here, this rotifer has been killed by a heliozoan,
destined to become food, a fate that this

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:13.840
flagellate is about to share as it becomes
captured by a heliozoan that is in the middle

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:15.520
of cell division.

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:21.920
The flagellate has been trapped by those long
extensions, called axopods, that radiate out

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:23.460
from the heliozoan’s body.

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:29.930
As the flagellate comes further in, it will
be engulfed by the cells into its own food

00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:32.430
compartment called a vacuole.

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:39.030
There, it will be lysed open and its contents
digested by the heliozoa.

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:42.760
In the end though, the natural order comes
for predators too.

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:50.200
Here, another heliozoan’s dying cellular
body attracts the various decomposers of the

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:54.110
microbial world.

00:03:54.110 --> 00:04:00.510
Aside from predators, there are many other
factors that lead a single-celled organism

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:01.790
to die.

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:08.680
Changes in temperature, oxygen concentration,
pH, water quality, so much more.

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:14.550
This single-celled organism is swollen because
the water surrounding it is entering the cell

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:16.650
via osmosis.

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:23.070
Many organisms have water pumps called contractile
vacuoles that they use to push water back

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:26.320
out and prevent that swelling.

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:32.380
But as in the case of this organism, sometimes
those contractile vacuoles stop working, and

00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:33.750
when that happens

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:37.860
the cell swells and explodes.

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:46.419
Other times, the cause of death is harder
to determine, like this Paradileptus that

00:04:46.419 --> 00:04:53.509
spent several hours swimming before going
still, its shape beginning to change until

00:04:53.509 --> 00:05:00.610
it melts away, seeming to kill not only the
Paradileptus but this small green cell swimming

00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:05.980
nearby…but leaving other smaller flagellates
seemingly unaffected.

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:13.860
And this brings us back to the beginning,
with our mystery organism that is about to

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:18.300
undergo a death laden with even more mysteries.

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:24.979
At first, the cell looks like it’s just
melting away, dissolving into something that

00:05:24.979 --> 00:05:33.479
resembles a microbial Milky Way…except that
for a few seconds, it almost looks like the

00:05:33.479 --> 00:05:37.620
cell membrane is able to close itself back
up.

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:42.919
We think, though we can’t know for sure,
that some of the mechanisms inside the cell

00:05:42.919 --> 00:05:47.949
are still working, and that the organism is
trying to recover.

00:05:47.949 --> 00:05:52.590
But alas, survival is not in the cards.

00:05:52.590 --> 00:05:58.909
Its membrane goes through lysis, releasing
its insides to the surrounding environment.

00:05:58.909 --> 00:06:05.660
This death is unlike any other kind of death
we have observed under our microscope, and

00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:08.999
we’re still not sure what caused it.

00:06:08.999 --> 00:06:13.659
Perhaps there were so many organisms in the
sample that they depleted the oxygen, and

00:06:13.659 --> 00:06:21.040
the organism could not continue cellular respiration,
but perhaps it was something else.

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:27.469
Death at every size holds its own mysteries,
but it also reveals.

00:06:27.469 --> 00:06:32.680
The observations we make, even the guesses
we come up with, tell us about the way these

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:38.650
microbes interact with their environment,
the way their bodies work, and the connections

00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:41.160
that exist between them.

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:47.499
It is only ever in the mysteries that knowledge
is waiting to be found.

00:06:47.499 --> 00:06:53.189
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds

00:06:53.189 --> 00:06:54.349
us.

00:06:54.349 --> 00:06:59.999
If you want to see more from our Master of
Microscopes, James, check out Jam and Germs

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.540
on Instagram where he is constantly posting
cool stuff.

00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:08.940
And if you want to see more from Journey to the Microcosmos here on YouTube

00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:13.460
there's always a subscribe button somewhere nearby.

